        content: "A dangerous architectural assumption across engineering teams is believing that a JWT inherently protects the privacy of the data it carries. Standard JWTs are strictly **JWS (JSON Web Signature, RFC 7515)**: they guarantee that claims have not been tampered with, but provide zero confidentiality.\n\n### The Need for JWE (JSON Web Encryption, RFC 7516)\nWhen an application must transmit highly sensitive identity data\u2014such as personally identifiable information (PII), banking details, or medical records\u2014within a token, relying on standard JWS exposes that data to any proxy, browser extension, or client inspecting the network tab. In these high-security environments, systems deploy **JWE**:\n- **Five Dot-Delimited Segments:** While a JWS possesses 3 segments, a compact JWE contains 5 base64url-encoded parts:\n  $$\\text{JWE} = \\text{Header} \\mathbin{\\mathbf{.}} \\text{EncryptedKey} \\mathbin{\\mathbf{.}} \\text{IV} \\mathbin{\\mathbf{.}} \\text{Ciphertext} \\mathbin{\\mathbf{.}} \\text{AuthTag}$$\n- **Authenticated Encryption:** JWE utilizes authenticated ciphers like **AES-256-GCM** or **ChaCha20-Poly1305**. The payload is fully encrypted; third parties inspecting the token see only opaque cryptographic ciphertext.\n\n### The Nested JWT Architecture (Defense in Depth)\nIn multi-tenant financial or healthcare networks, the industry gold standard is **Nested JWTs**:\n1. First, the identity server constructs the claims and signs them with its private RSA/ECDSA key, producing a standard signed JWS (proving authenticity).\n2. Second, the entire JWS string is encapsulated as the payload of a JWE container and encrypted using the downstream resource server's public key (providing confidentiality).\n3. When received, the resource server decrypts the JWE using its private key and then verifies the inner JWS signature using the identity provider's public key, satisfying both non-repudiation and end-to-end data privacy."
